Chapter: DeviceBuffer

 A DeviceBuffer is a handle to GPU-resident memory. DeviceArg wraps
 arguments for kernel launch: read-only buffer ids, mutable buffer
 ids, or scalar values. KernelDescriptor identifies a compiled
 kernel by name and target architecture.

 The Allocator section below turns the handle into a usable buffer.
 It is a bump allocator over the GPU command-buffer window
 [0xBE000000, 0xBF000000) -- the same window the rasterizer commands
 travel through, and the authority the Gpu.Memory capability guards.
 Every operation goes through gpu-mem-write / gpu-mem-read, so every
 caller must declare Gpu.Memory and earn bit 18; the type is what
 makes that requirement visible. This exists so a caller stops
 hand-poking the window to work around the absence of an allocator.

 We say:

Section: Types

  DeviceBuffer = record {
    db-id : Integer,
    db-size : Integer
  }

  DeviceArg =
    | ArgBuf
    | ArgBufMut
    | ArgScalar

  KernelDescriptor = record {
    kd-name : Text,
    kd-sm-target : Integer,
    kd-ptx-size : Integer
  }

Section: Allocator

 The window constants. The cursor -- the next free absolute address --
 lives in the first word of the window itself, so the allocator needs
 no kernel metadata cell and no borrowed state. A fresh window reads
 zero there, which db-norm-cursor lifts to the first address past the
 reserved header.

  db-window-base : Integer = #BE000000
  db-window-top : Integer = #BF000000
  db-header : Integer = 64

  db-round8 : Integer -> Integer
  db-round8 (n) = (n + 7) / 8 * 8

  db-norm-cursor : Integer -> Integer
  db-norm-cursor (c) = if c < db-window-base + db-header then db-window-base + db-header else c

  db-read-cursor : Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] Integer
  db-read-cursor (d) = db-norm-cursor (gpu-mem-read db-window-base 0)

 Allocate n bytes. The returned db-id is the absolute address of the
 buffer, directly usable as a pointer; db-size is the requested size.
 An allocation that would overflow the window returns the null buffer
 (db-id 0) rather than a handle onto memory it does not own. The
 effectful cursor write is fed to db-mk as an argument it ignores, the
 way GpuRender keeps effectful writes out of let-binding position.

  db-null : DeviceBuffer
  db-null = DeviceBuffer { db-id = 0, db-size = 0 }

  db-mk : Integer, Integer, Integer -> DeviceBuffer
  db-mk (cur) (n) (w) = DeviceBuffer { db-id = cur, db-size = n }

  db-alloc-at : Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] DeviceBuffer
  db-alloc-at (cur) (n) =
    if cur + db-round8 n > db-window-top then db-null
    else db-mk cur n (gpu-mem-write db-window-base 0 (cur + db-round8 n))

  device-buffer-alloc : Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] DeviceBuffer
  device-buffer-alloc (n) = db-alloc-at (db-read-cursor 0) n

 Allocate a buffer sized to the host words and copy them in, one
 32-bit word per slot. A null buffer (the window is full) is returned
 without writing, so the copy never lands on an address the allocator
 does not hold.

  db-write-words : Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] Integer
  db-write-words (base) (words) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else gpu-mem-write base (i * 4) (list-at words i) + db-write-words base words (i + 1) n

  db-ret : DeviceBuffer, Integer -> DeviceBuffer
  db-ret (buf) (w) = buf

  db-after-write : DeviceBuffer, List Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] DeviceBuffer
  db-after-write (buf) (words) =
    if buf.db-id == 0 then buf
    else db-ret buf (db-write-words (buf.db-id) words 0 (list-length words))

  device-buffer-from-host : List Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] DeviceBuffer
  device-buffer-from-host (words) = db-after-write (device-buffer-alloc (list-length words * 4)) words

 Read a buffer back to a host word list. A null or empty buffer reads
 nothing.

  db-read-words : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] List Integer
  db-read-words (base) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else db-read-words base (i + 1) n (list-push acc (gpu-mem-read base (i * 4)))

  device-buffer-to-host : DeviceBuffer -> [Gpu.Memory] List Integer
  device-buffer-to-host (buf) = db-read-words (buf.db-id) 0 (buf.db-size / 4) []

 Free is LIFO: a buffer that is the top of the bump stack rewinds the
 cursor and its space is reused by the next allocation; any other
 buffer is a no-op, which is the honest behaviour of a bump allocator
 rather than a pretence of general free.

  db-free-at : DeviceBuffer, Integer -> [Gpu.Memory] Integer
  db-free-at (buf) (cur) =
    if buf.db-id + db-round8 (buf.db-size) == cur then gpu-mem-write db-window-base 0 (buf.db-id)
    else 0

  device-buffer-free : DeviceBuffer -> [Gpu.Memory] Integer
  device-buffer-free (buf) = db-free-at buf (db-read-cursor 0)

